<problem>
<!-- <title>Applications of integration &#x2D; average value</title> -->

<setup>


<function name="f" variables="t">t</function>
<function name="g" variables="t" symbolic="true">e^(2t)</function>
<math name="lb">0</math>
<math name="ub">3</math>
<function name="integral_g" variables="t" symbolic="true">(1/2)e^(2t) </function>
<math name="ave_g" simplify="full">$integral_g /($ub -$lb )</math>
<math name="average_g_evaluated" simplify="full">($$integral_g($ub)-$$integral_g($lb))/($ub -$lb)</math>
<evaluate name="intg0" forceNumeric="true" function="$integral_g" input="0" />
<evaluate name="intg3" forceNumeric="true" function="$integral_g" input="3" />
<number name="numeric_ave_g_eval">($intg3-$intg0)/($ub -$lb )</number>
<math name="integral_g_eval" simplify="full">$$integral_g($ub)-$$integral_g($lb) </math>

  
<function name="h" variables="t" symbolic="true"> 10t^2+20t+10</function>
<function name="integral_h" variables="t">(10/3)*t^3+10*t^2+10t</function>
<math name="ave_h" simplify="full">$integral_h /14</math>
<math name="ave_h_eval" simplify="full">($$integral_h(14))/14 </math>
<math name="integral_h_eval">$$integral_h(14) </math>
<evaluate name="inth14" forceNumeric="true" function="$integral_h" input="14" />
<number name="numeric_ave_h_eval">$inth14/14</number>
<evaluate name="sym_h_eval" function="$integral_h" forceSymbolic="true" input="14" />
<math name="sym_ave_h_eval" simplify="full">($sym_h_eval)/14</math>

</setup>

<p>
We can use definite integrals to help us find averages.</p>

<!--Problem #1*******************************************-->
<exercise includeAutoNumber="true"><title />
<p>Suppose we know that the volume of a cell is changing at a rate of <m>f(t)</m> <m>\mu {\rm m}^3/s</m> and we want to know the average rate from <m>t=a</m> to <m>t=b</m> seconds.  The average rate of change is given by 
<answer inline="true">
<choiceInput>
<choice credit="1">the total change divided by the time that has passed</choice>
<choice>the time that has passed divided by the total change</choice>
<choice>the total change times the time that has passed</choice>
</choiceInput>
</answer>
.  Since the total change is given by 
<answer inline="true">
<choiceInput>
<choice credit="1">the definite integral of f</choice>
<choice>the indefinite integral of f</choice>
<choice>the derivative of f</choice>
</choiceInput>
</answer>
 and the time that has passed is given by 

<answer name="diff1">b-a</answer>

, we can write down a formula for the average rate of change:
</p>

<p><m>\displaystyle  \frac{1}{<copy prop="submittedResponse" source="diff1" componentType="math" />}
\int_{ <math extend="$a1.immediateValue" /> }^{ <math extend="$b1.immediateValue" /> }</m>  
<mathInput name="u_f" />
 <m>dt</m>, where integration limits are from 
<mathInput name="a1" />
to 
<mathInput name="b1" />.
<answer name="ansAve1">
<award matchPartial="true" referencesAreResponses="$a1 $b1">
  <when>(<mathInput isResponse="true" extend="$u_f" />=f(t) or $u_f=f) and <when>$a1=a and $b1=b</when>
</when></award>
</answer>
</p>



<p>For any function <m>f(t)</m>, the formula gives us the average value of <m>f(t)</m> between <m>t=a</m> and <m>t=b</m>.  Suppose we want to know the average volume of the cell between <m>t=a</m> and <m>t=b</m>, where the volume at time <m>t</m> is given by <m>V(t)</m>.  The formula for this is:
</p>

<p><mathInput name="ab2" />
<m>\displaystyle  \int_{ <math extend="$a2.immediateValue" /> }^{ <math extend="$b2.immediateValue" /> }</m>  
<mathInput name="u_V" />
 <m>dt</m>, where integration limits are from 
<mathInput name="a2" />
to 
<mathInput name="b2" />.
<answer name="ansAve2">
<award matchPartial="true" referencesAreResponses="$ab2 $a2 $b2">
  <when>$ab2 = 1/(b-a) and (<mathInput isResponse="true" extend="$u_V" />=V(t) or $u_V=V) and <when>$a2=a and $b2=b</when>
</when></award>
</answer>
</p>
</exercise>


<!--Problem #2********************************************-->
<exercise includeAutoNumber="true"><title />
<p>
Find the average value of the function <m>g(t)=<math extend="$g.formula" /></m> between <m>t= $lb </m> and <m>t=$ub </m>.  First write down the expression involving an integral that will give the average value, then evaluate it.
</p>

<p>The integration limits are from <mathInput name="a3" />
to 
<mathInput name="b3" /> 
<answer matchPartial="true">
  <award referencesAreResponses="$a3 $b4">
    <when>$a3=0 and $b3=3</when>
  </award>
</answer>.</p>

<p>Average value = <mathInput name="ab3" />
<m>\displaystyle  \int_{ <math extend="$a3.immediateValue" /> }^{ <math extend="$b3.immediateValue" /> }</m>  
<mathInput name="u_g" />
 <m>dt</m>
<answer name="ansAve3">
<award matchPartial="true" referencesAreResponses="$ab3 $u_g">
  <when>$ab3 = 1/3 and $u_g=$g</when>
</award>
</answer>
</p>
  

<p>
<m> = </m> 
<answer>
<award symbolicEquality="true" simplifyOnCompare="true"> $average_g_evaluated </award>
<award credit="0.9" name="award12" allowedErrorInNumbers="0.001">$numeric_ave_g_eval </award>
<award symbolicEquality="true" simplifyOnCompare="true" credit="0" name="award13">$integral_g_eval </award>
</answer>
</p>
<feedback condition="$award12">For full credit, don't round your answer but leave it in terms of <c>e^()</c> and/or a fraction.</feedback>
<feedback condition="$award13">Don't forget to divide by the change in time.</feedback>
</exercise>


<!--Problem#3*************************************************-->
<exercise includeAutoNumber="true"><title />
<p>A disease is infecting people at a rate of <m><math extend="$h.formula" /></m> people per day, where <m>t</m> gives time in days.  Write down an expression involving an integral that gives the average rate of infection over the first two weeks and evaluate it. 
</p>

<p>The integration limits are from <mathInput name="a4" />
to 
<mathInput name="b4" />.
<answer matchPartial="true" numAwardsCredited="2">
  <award referencesAreResponses="$a4 $b4">
    <when>$a4=0 and $b4=14</when>
  </award>
  <award credit="0" name="weeks">
    <when>$b4=2</when>
  </award>
</answer>.</p>
<feedback condition="$weeks">Since <m>t</m> is in days, enter expression in terms of days, not weeks.
</feedback>
  
<p>Average value = <mathInput name="ab4" />
<m>\displaystyle  \int_{ <math extend="$a4.immediateValue" /> }^{ <math extend="$b4.immediateValue" /> }</m>  
<mathInput name="u_h" />
 <m>dt</m>
<answer name="ansAve4">
<award matchPartial="true" referencesAreResponses="$ab4 $u_g">
  <when>$ab4 = 1/14 and $u_h=$h</when>
</award>
</answer>
</p>
  

<p>
<m> = </m> 
<answer>
<award symbolicEquality="true" simplifyOnCompare="true"> $ave_h_eval </award>
<award credit="0.9" name="award14" allowedErrorInNumbers="0.1">$numeric_ave_h_eval </award>
<award symbolicEquality="true" simplifyOnCompare="true" credit="0" name="award15">$integral_h_eval </award>
</answer></p>
<feedback condition="$award14">For full credit, don't round your answer but leave it in terms of <c>e^()</c> and/or a fraction.</feedback>
<feedback condition="$award15">Don't forget to divide by the change in time.</feedback>


<hint>
Since the infection rate <m><math extend="$h.formula" /></m> and time <m>t</m> are given in days, be sure to think in terms of days, not weeks.
</hint>
</exercise>



  
<solution>
<ol>
<li>
<p>The average rate of change of a function is given by the total change divided by the total time that has passed.  We have seen how to find the total change by evaluating a definite integral, namely <m>\displaystyle \int_a^b f(t)dt</m>, and the total time that has passed is just <m>b-a</m>.  So the average rate of change is <m>\displaystyle \frac{1}{b-a} \int_a^b f(t)dt</m>.</p>

<p>The average rate of change is the average of the function <m>f(t)</m>.  We can use the same formula to determine the average value of any function <m>F</m>: <m>\displaystyle \frac{1}{b-a} \int_a^b F(t)\, dt</m> gives the average value of <m>F(t)</m> from <m>a</m> to <m>b</m>.</p>
</li>

<li>
The average value of the function <m>g(t)=<math extend="$g.formula" /></m> between <m>t= $lb </m> and <m>t=$ub </m> is given by
<me>\frac{1}{ $ub -$lb  } \int_{$lb}^{$ub} $g dt =\frac{1}{ $ub -$lb } \displaystyle \left($integral_g \right) \Bigg|_0^3 = $average_g_evaluated </me>
</li>

<li>
The average rate of infection over the first two weeks is given by
<me>\frac{1}{14-0} \int_{0}^{14} $h dt =\frac{1}{14 - 0} \displaystyle \left($integral_h \right) \Bigg|_0^{14} = $sym_ave_h_eval </me>
  
</li>


</ol>

</solution>


</problem>